  <h1>What's Actually Included in a Boiler Service? The Full York Checklist, No Jargon</h1>
  <p>Boiler servicing is the second biggest reason people call us, and the question underneath most of those calls is really the same one: what am I actually paying for? Fair question. A lot of people have had the experience of an engineer in and out in twenty minutes, sticking a sticker on the boiler and taking the money. Here's what a proper service actually involves, step by step, so you can tell the difference.</p>

  <h2>What we actually check</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Full visual inspection:</strong> the boiler, the pipework around it, and anywhere water or gas could be escaping without you noticing.</li>
    <li><strong>Flue and ventilation check.</strong> Exhaust gases, including carbon monoxide, need to actually get out of your house rather than build up where they shouldn't.</li>
    <li><strong>Gas pressure and burner check:</strong> confirming the boiler's burning gas at the right pressure. Run it too lean or too rich and it wastes gas or wears parts out faster.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety device test.</strong> These are the devices that shut the boiler down if something goes wrong, so they get tested every time rather than assumed to be fine.</li>
    <li><strong>Casing and seals inspection:</strong> checking nothing's cracked, loose, or letting gas or fumes escape where it shouldn't.</li>
    <li><strong>Full strip-down and clean of the heat exchanger.</strong> This is the part that actually heats your water, and debris builds up in here over a year. It's the single biggest cause of boilers running inefficiently or breaking down early.</li>
    <li><strong>Condenser trap cleared out.</strong> A blocked condensate pipe is one of the most common reasons a boiler trips out in cold weather, and an easy thing to miss if nobody's looking.</li>
    <li><strong>CO alarm check:</strong> if you've got one fitted, we'll check it's actually working, not just present.</li>
    <li><strong>Written service record.</strong> Proof of what was checked, dated and kept for your own records and for any warranty claim down the line.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why the heat exchanger clean matters more than people think</h2>
  <p>Most of what causes a boiler to fail isn't a dramatic fault, it's slow build-up inside parts you never see. A heat exchanger that's never been stripped down and cleaned has to work harder every year to do the same job, and that's what eventually shows up as a breakdown on the coldest night of the year rather than a quiet fix in autumn. This is the part of a service that takes the time, and it's also the part that gets skipped entirely by anyone doing the twenty-minute version.</p>

  <h2>What it costs, and what you get for it</h2>
  <p>A one-off <a href="/boiler-servicing/">boiler service York</a> homeowners book with us is £120, and that covers everything on the list above, not a stripped-down version of it. You'll get a written record at the end, so if a manufacturer ever queries a warranty claim, you've got proof the <a href="/advice-centre/do-i-need-annual-service/">annual boiler service York</a> warranty terms require actually happened, and what was found when it did. If you're booking this every year anyway, which most people end up doing, our <a href="/boiler-care-plans/">Care Plan</a> works out cheaper over time and throws in a free CO alarm.</p>

  <h2>Landlords, this applies to you too</h2>
  <p>If you're a landlord, the annual check isn't optional, it's a legal requirement, and it covers some of the same ground as a service plus the gas safety certificate itself. If you're not sure how a standard service differs from a <a href="/advice-centre/cp12-landlord-certificate/">CP12 landlord certificate</a>, that's worth reading separately, since the two get confused a lot.</p>

  <h2>Booking an honest service in York</h2>
